      ],
      "text" : "8.8"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 16.936400000000003,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        177.60000000000002,
        722.00283639999998,
        239.77498813668416,
        16.919463599999972
      ],
      "text" : "Radicals - Complex Numbers"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960200000000015,
        688.07140000000004,
        425.20186071016963,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Objective: Add, subtract, multiply, rationalize, and simplify expres-"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        673.67140000000006,
        175.56953266795779,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "sions using complex numbers."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        652.89924480000002,
        425.21697343987205,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "World View Note: When mathematics was first used, the primary purpose was"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        638.49924480000004,
        425.2183022048257,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "for counting. Thus they did not originally use negatives, zero, fractions or irra-"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        624.09924480000006,
        425.12791252426246,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "tional numbers. However, the ancient Egyptians quickly developed the need for “a"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        609.69924480000009,
        425.20277332400644,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "part” and so they made up a new type of number, the ratio or fraction. The"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        595.29924480000011,
        425.15647053232641,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "Ancient Greeks did not believe in irrational numbers (people were killed for"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        580.89924480000013,
        425.15778372503041,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "believing otherwise). The Mayans of Central America later made up the number"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        566.49924480000016,
        425.21748342215687,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "zero when they found use for it as a placeholder. Ancient Chinese Mathematicians"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        552.09924480000018,
        299.65386002426885,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "made up negative numbers when they found use for them."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        531.33904480000012,
        425.24807806586881,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "In mathematics, when the current number system does not provide the tools to"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        516.93904480000015,
        425.15744975616008,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "solve the problems the culture is working with, we tend to make up new ways for"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        502.53904480000011,
        425.21625820651525,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "dealing with the problem that can solve the problem. Throughout history this has"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        488.13904480000014,
        425.24809862115842,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "been the case with the need for a number that is nothing (0), smaller than zero"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        473.73904480000016,
        425.22030621765123,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "(negatives), between integers (fractions), and between fractions (irrational num-"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        459.33904480000018,
        425.22212862781441,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "bers). This is also the case for the square roots of negative numbers. To work"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        444.9390448000002,
        425.20353278333448,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "with the square root of negative numbers mathematicians have defined what are"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        430.53904480000023,
        204.01253587855365,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "called imaginary and complex numbers."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        122.99999999999999,
        393.74211200000025,
        327.61077760000001,
        20.922060380000005
      ],
      "text" : "Definition of Imaginary Numbers: i2 =− 1 (thus i=− 1"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        419.3999,
        401.9792448,
        37.036477600000069,
        20.716096000000277
      ],
      "text" : "√ )"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        365.06280800000002,
        288.03248841000004,
        23.574884499999996
      ],
      "text" : "Examples of imaginary numbers include 3i,− 6i, 3"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 7.9701100000000009,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        368.76036000000011,
        369.74728250000004,
        66.697567999999933,
        15.771072699999991
      ],
      "text" : "5 i and 3i 5"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        419.63972000000007,
        373.53924480000001,
        90.501555678143916,
        21.92731520000001
      ],
      "text" : "√ . A complex"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959799999999973,
        358.2994448,
        393.8515744,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "number is one that contains both a real and imaginary part, such as 2+ 5i."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959559999999954,
        337.53964480000002,
        425.17372677555198,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "With this definition, the square root of a negative number is no longer undefined."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959559999999954,
        323.13964480000004,
        425.17356410058238,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "We now are allowed to do basic operations with the square root of negatives."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959559999999954,
        308.73964480000006,
        425.2219120626944,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "First we will consider exponents on imaginary numbers. We will do this by"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959559999999954,
        294.33964480000009,
        168.51189840999996,
        13.298257700000022
      ],
      "text" : "manipulating our definition of i2"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        256.07955999999996,
        285.86340800000011,
        254.06467836802568,
        20.455347200000006
      ],
      "text" : "=− 1. If we multiply both sides of the definition"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959539999999976,
        271.46340800000013,
        425.21884035563528,
        21.774494500000003
      ],
      "text" : "by i, the equation becomes i3 =− i. Then if we multiply both sides of the equa-"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95969999999997,
        265.53964480000013,
        211.47216841000002,
        13.298257700000022
      ],
      "text" : "tion again by i, the equation becomes i4"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        299.87981999999994,
        257.06340800000015,
        33.512188409999965,
        21.774494500000003
      ],
      "text" : "=− i2"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        336.83956999999992,
        257.06340800000015,
        173.29202440000006,
        21.774494500000003
      ],
      "text" : "=− (− 1) = 1, or simply i4 = 1."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        242.30320800000015,
        425.21502554762236,
        21.654574499999967
      ],
      "text" : "Multiplying again by i gives i5 = i. One more time gives i6 = i2 =− 1. And if this"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959889999999916,
        236.37944480000016,
        425.20108071347204,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "pattern continues we see a cycle forming, the exponents on i change we cycle"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959889999999916,
        213.50320800000014,
        425.20109941376001,
        20.455347199999977
      ],
      "text" : "through simplified answers of i,− 1,− i, 1. As there are 4 diﬀerent possible"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959679999999878,
        207.57944480000015,
        425.17257780421124,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "answers in this cycle, if we divide the exponent by 4 and consider the remainder,"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959679999999878,
        193.17944480000011,
        393.4927692246784,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "we can simplify any exponent on i by learning just the following four values:"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
